## 3.1 Execution Trace

### Input

```python
arr = [2, 7, 11, 15]
target = 26
```

### Correct Execution Trace

|  i |  j | arr[i] | arr[j] | Sum | Match? | Loop Action      |
| -: | -: | -----: | -----: | --: | :----: | ---------------- |
|  0 |  1 |      2 |      7 |   9 |    ❌   | Continue         |
|  0 |  2 |      2 |     11 |  13 |    ❌   | Continue         |
|  0 |  3 |      2 |     15 |  17 |    ❌   | Move to next `i` |
|  1 |  2 |      7 |     11 |  18 |    ❌   | Continue         |
|  1 |  3 |      7 |     15 |  22 |    ❌   | Move to next `i` |
|  2 |  3 |     11 |     15 |  26 |    ✅   | Return `(2, 3)`  |

The valid pair is finally found at:

```text
i = 2
j = 3
```

Therefore:

```text
11 + 15 = 26
```

and the function returns:

```python
(2, 3)
```

> **Important:** In the buggy implementation, the search stops after the first three rows because `return None` is executed immediately after `i = 0`. Therefore, rows 4–6 are never reached.

