## 8. Time and Space Complexity Analysis

### Time Complexity

For an array containing `n` elements, the number of unique pairs is:

[
\frac{n(n-1)}{2}
]

Therefore:

[
T(n) = O(n^2)
]

The `j = i + 1` optimization removes redundant comparisons but does not change the asymptotic complexity.

### Space Complexity

The algorithm uses only a fixed number of variables:

```text
i
j
n
```

No additional array, hash map, or recursion is used.

Therefore:

[
S(n) = O(1)
]

### Complexity Summary

| Case         | Complexity |
| ------------ | ---------- |
| Best Case    | O(1)       |
| Average Case | O(n²)      |
| Worst Case   | O(n²)      |
| Space        | O(1)       |

### Best Case

The first pair checked satisfies the target:

```text
arr[0] + arr[1] == target
```

Therefore, the algorithm returns immediately.

### Worst Case

No valid pair exists.

The algorithm must check all:

[
\frac{n(n-1)}{2}
]

possible combinations.

Hence:

```text
O(n²)
```

