## 9. Conclusion

The original Pair Sum implementation failed because the statement:

```python
return None
```

was placed inside the outer `for` loop.

As a result, the function stopped after checking only the first outer-loop iteration and incorrectly reported failure when a valid pair existed later in the array.

The corrected implementation moves `return None` outside both loops, ensuring that all possible pairs are examined before concluding that no solution exists.

Additionally, using:

```python
range(i + 1, n)
```

prevents self-comparisons and eliminates redundant mirror comparisons such as `(i, j)` and `(j, i)`.

The final algorithm remains a pure brute-force solution with:

```text
Time Complexity  : O(n²)
Space Complexity : O(1)
```

This provides a correct, efficient, and logically structured brute-force solution to the Pair Sum problem.
